            <ArticleTitle>Abdominal myomectomy after failed uterine artery embolization.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1842</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Report a case of a difficult myomectomy after a failed uterine artery embolization (UAE).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A university medical center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 30-year-old woman with pelvic pain and menorrhagia secondary to an enlarging 18- to 20-week-size fibroid uterus.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Abdominal myomectomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Complicated myomectomy after UAE.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A patient underwent a difficult myomectomy after failed UAE. The myomectomy was only partially completed due to the difficult dissection of the myomas.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Myomectomy after UAE may be unusually difficult due to the degenerative changes that occur within the leiomyomas.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
